CAT = "phcleProcedure"; SEG = "PRO"
# Criminal Law and Jurisprudence: the criminal justice system, human rights, evidence,
# criminal procedure and court testimony (Rules of Court; 2019 amendments to the Rules on Evidence).
Q = [
("The five pillars of the Philippine criminal justice system are law enforcement, prosecution, courts, corrections and:",
 "The community", ["The legislature", "The media", "The barangay tanod"],
 "The community is the fifth pillar: it reports crime, serves as witness and helps reintegrate offenders. The barangay tanod is part of the community pillar, not a pillar of its own."),
("A peace officer may arrest a person without a warrant when an offense has just been committed and he has:",
 "Probable cause from personal knowledge of facts", ["A tip from an anonymous caller", "A suspicion based on the person's criminal record", "An order from the barangay captain"],
 "Rule 113, Section 5(b) allows a hot pursuit arrest when an offense has just been committed and the officer has probable cause, based on personal knowledge of facts or circumstances, that the person committed it."),
("A search warrant is valid for:",
 "10 days from its date", ["30 days from its date", "5 days from its date", "Until it is served"],
 "Rule 126, Section 10 says a search warrant is valid for ten days from its date; after that it is void. The issuing court may issue a new one."),
("Which is a requisite for issuing a search warrant?",
 "Probable cause personally determined by the judge", ["Written approval of the provincial chief of police", "A prior criminal complaint in court", "Consent of the property owner"],
 "Article III, Section 2 of the 1987 Constitution requires probable cause determined personally by the judge after examining the complainant and witnesses under oath, and a particular description of the place and things."),
("Evidence addressed to the senses of the court, such as a knife shown to the judge, is:",
 "Object evidence", ["Documentary evidence", "Testimonial evidence", "Circumstantial evidence"],
 "Rule 130 calls evidence addressed to the senses object or real evidence. Documentary evidence offers writings for their contents; testimonial evidence is given by a witness."),
("Under the 2019 amendments to the Rules on Evidence, the best evidence rule is now called the:",
 "Original document rule", ["Primary evidence rule", "Parol evidence rule", "Authentication rule"],
 "A.M. No. 19-08-15-SC renamed the best evidence rule the original document rule: when the subject of inquiry is the contents of a document, the original must be produced, subject to exceptions."),
("A statement made by a person under consciousness of impending death, about the cause and circumstances of his death, is admissible as a:",
 "Dying declaration", ["Res gestae statement", "Declaration against interest", "Admission by silence"],
 "A dying declaration is an exception to the hearsay rule when the declarant was conscious of impending death, the statement concerns the cause and circumstances of death, and he would have been competent to testify."),
("The quantum of proof needed to convict an accused is:",
 "Proof beyond reasonable doubt", ["Preponderance of evidence", "Substantial evidence", "Clear and convincing evidence"],
 "Conviction requires proof beyond reasonable doubt, moral certainty of guilt. Preponderance of evidence applies in civil cases and substantial evidence in administrative cases."),
("An extrajudicial confession of an accused is not sufficient for conviction unless corroborated by evidence of:",
 "The corpus delicti", ["The motive", "Flight from the scene", "A prior conviction"],
 "Rule 133 requires an extrajudicial confession to be corroborated by evidence of the corpus delicti, proof that the crime was actually committed."),
("A preliminary investigation is required before filing an information for an offense punishable by imprisonment of at least:",
 "4 years, 2 months and 1 day", ["6 years and 1 day", "1 year and 1 day", "6 years, 2 months and 1 day"],
 "Rule 112, Section 1 requires preliminary investigation for offenses punishable by at least four years, two months and one day, regardless of the fine."),
("Before conviction, bail is a matter of right except when the offense is punishable by reclusion perpetua or life imprisonment and:",
 "Evidence of guilt is strong", ["The accused has a prior arrest", "The victim objects to bail", "The accused is a public officer"],
 "Rule 114 makes bail a right before conviction except for offenses punishable by death, reclusion perpetua or life imprisonment when evidence of guilt is strong. Then bail is discretionary after a hearing."),
("Under Article III, Section 12 of the 1987 Constitution, a person under investigation for an offense has the right to:",
 "Independent counsel of his own choice", ["Be released after six hours of questioning", "A jury trial", "Refuse fingerprinting"],
 "Section 12 guarantees the rights to remain silent and to competent and independent counsel, preferably of his own choice, which he can waive only in writing and in the presence of counsel."),
("The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted by the UN General Assembly in:",
 "1948", ["1945", "1966", "1987"],
 "The UDHR was adopted on 10 December 1948, now marked as Human Rights Day. 1945 is the UN Charter; 1966 is the two international covenants."),
("The Commission on Human Rights of the Philippines was created by:",
 "The 1987 Constitution", ["R.A. 6975", "The Revised Penal Code", "A presidential decree of 1972"],
 "Article XIII, Section 17 of the 1987 Constitution created the Commission on Human Rights as an independent office to investigate human rights violations involving civil and political rights."),
("A motion to quash may be filed by the accused:",
 "Before entering his plea", ["After the prosecution rests", "Only after judgment", "Only during the pre-trial conference"],
 "Under Rule 117 the accused may move to quash the complaint or information at any time before entering his plea. Grounds include lack of jurisdiction and double jeopardy."),
("Under the Rule on Examination of a Child Witness, a child witness is a person below the age of:",
 "18", ["15", "12", "21"],
 "A.M. No. 004-07-SC defines a child witness as any person under 18 at the time of giving testimony, and allows facilitators, live-link testimony and other protective measures."),
]
